Self-limiting mild pityriasis versicolor on the upper back causing cosmetic concern
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Well-demarcated truncal macules, Fine provoked scaling, Spaghetti and meatball appearance on KOH

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-035
Reason for Visit: New onset of a spreading truncal rash requiring assessment to exclude secondary syphilis, guttate psoriasis, or severe fungal infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a rapidly expanding, discolored rash on the torso. Urgent clinical evaluation was mandated to physically examine the lesions, assess for systemic signs, and confidently rule out highly infectious etiologies like secondary syphilis or progressing severe cutaneous diseases prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I have these strange light and dark spots spreading all over my back, and they itch slightly when I sweat.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes multiple well-demarcated hyperpigmented or hypopigmented macules on the upper trunk, evolving over the past few weeks. Notes the coalescence of smaller macules into larger patches. Reports mild localized pruritus explicitly exacerbated by sweating during exercise. Mentions prominent visibility of the lesions following recent sun exposure. Complete absence of systemic inflammatory signs, fever, or joint pain. Intact general health without recent risky sexual contact.
Functional Status: Fully independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: High cosmetic distress regarding the appearance of the skin.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 122/78 | HR: 74 | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 14 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, focused entirely on the cosmetic appearance of the back.
Physical Exam: Skin examination reveals well-demarcated truncal macules (hypo/hyperpigmented). Fine dusty epidermal scaling is provoked by gentle scraping (Besnier's sign). Diagnostic triad confirmed historically and clinically: well-demarcated truncal macules, fine provoked scaling, and expected classic microscopic appearance. Complete sparing of the palms and soles. Normal underlying cutaneous sensation. Absence of systemic inflammatory signs. No regional or generalized lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: High ambient humidity; patient wearing synthetic, non-breathable athletic wear.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current immunosuppressive medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Self-limiting mild pityriasis versicolor on the upper back causing cosmetic concern - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Secondary Syphilis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete sparing of the palms and soles, absence of generalized lymphadenopathy, and presence of fine provoked scaling.

Guttate Psoriasis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of preceding streptococcal infection, lesions are macules rather than thick, erythematous, silvery-scaled papules.

Vitiligo - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of scaling upon scraping (vitiligo is perfectly smooth), and macules are not purely depigmented but show variable pigment changes.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a spreading truncal dermatosis. It was critical to perform a face-to-face assessment to inspect the palms and soles and evaluate for systemic involvement, thereby safely ruling out secondary syphilis or other rapidly progressive systemic inflammatory conditions. Delaying the assessment could lead to significant patient distress or missed infectious pathology. Clinical confirmation of a benign, superficial yeast overgrowth provided immediate reassurance and avoided unnecessary systemic workups.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescription for topical antifungal therapy (e.g., 2% ketoconazole shampoo used as a body wash). Instruction to apply the lather to the affected areas, leave it on for 5-10 minutes, and then rinse off, daily for 1-2 weeks.
Positive Behavioral Change: Shower immediately after vigorous exercise and switch to wearing loose, breathable cotton clothing, eliminating the hot, humid microclimate that allows the yeast to proliferate.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review if the scaling fails to resolve after 2 weeks of compliant topical therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you develop a sudden high fever, if the rash rapidly turns into painful purple blisters, or if you experience difficulty breathing.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a benign yeast overgrowth; pigmentary changes can take months to fully resolve even after the organism is eradicated.
Clinical Course: The mild itching and scaling will stop within 1 to 2 weeks of treatment. The skin discoloration will slowly fade over 2 to 3 months as the skin naturally replaces itself.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if the spots begin to form open sores, or if you develop a widespread rash involving your palms and the soles of your feet.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A yeast that naturally lives on everyone's skin overgrew due to trapped sweat and heat. The yeast produces an acid that temporarily bleaching the skin, causing the light spots. The medicated wash kills the yeast.
Actionable Steps: Lather the medicated shampoo over your entire torso and wait 10 minutes before rinsing. Do not expect the skin color to return to normal immediately; this takes months.

SMS INFORMATION

